-- scripts/dst/seeded_generator_dst.ail
--
-- WI-A13 stage 4: D2's SEEDED GENERATOR, against the real driver.
--
-- Stage 2 built discovery's recording half and ran it against hand-authored
-- worlds. This stage builds the CHOOSING half — the schema-versioned generator
-- that "chooses a compatible response, fault, and latency from that request
-- plus explicit generator state" — and grades it.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT THIS ASSERTS, AND WHY EACH AXIS IS HERE
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
--   A. SEED SENSITIVITY, AND IT IS THIS STAGE'S WHOLE RISK. Changing the seed
--      changes the program; changing nothing else changes nothing. The failure
--      it catches is a generator that ignores its seed — which is perfectly
--      deterministic, structurally valid, strictly replayable, and green under
--      every other gate in this project. Determinism is 0-for-19 across nine
--      clusters and would be 0-for-20 here, because a generator that ignores
--      its seed is MORE reproducible, not less.
--
--      The rule is CONTENT-shaped, not count-shaped, and the axis carries its
--      own anti-count control: at least one differently-seeded pair must have
--      the SAME interaction count and different recorded outcomes. Without that
--      the whole axis could be reading program LENGTH, which cluster 9's site
--      19 established a count is blind to. Seeds 9 and 13 are that pair, and
--      their class censuses are identical down to the last interaction —
--      2 provider, 1 tool ok, 1 approval ok, 1 env ok, 2 chunked, 4 clock —
--      so nothing but the recorded outcomes can tell them apart.
--
--   B. THE MUTANT, AND IT IS A REAL RUN. `generate` is given a seed to BUILD
--      the world with and a seed to RECORD on the row; the mutant rows pass a
--      constant to the first and three different values to the second.
--      It is the same driver, the same recorder, the same program shape; only
--      the generator's response to its seed is broken. Axis A must go red on
--      it, naming `generator-ignores-its-seed`. This is the executable form of
--      "written before the generator, proven red against a seed-ignoring stub",
--      and it stays in the suite rather than being a demonstration that was run
--      once.
--
--   C. THE GENERATOR WAS ACTUALLY CONSULTED. Every cursor of a generated world
--      starts EMPTY, and `gen.draws` must have grown. A generating adapter that
--      silently fell through to its recording counterpart would leave the world
--      serving defaults consistently, and consistently is all a determinism
--      check asks.
--
--   D. DECLARED BOUNDS (D2). On the honest bounds, zero generator failures. On
--      a deliberately tightened one, the named failure fires AND the run stays
--      bounded — both halves of "exceeding a bound is a generator/harness
--      failure, NOT an unbounded test run".
--
--   E. THE GENERATED PROGRAM IS A VALID PROGRAM. Stage 1's pure structural
--      validator, and stage 1's declared-bounds check, over a program no human
--      authored.
--
--   F. THE GENERATED PROGRAM STRICTLY REPLAYS. Stage 3's reconstitution
--      balance, its walk, and its terminal outcome. This is the strongest
--      available sign that the generator is CHOOSING rather than INVENTING: a
--      generated request that production control flow never made would show up
--      as an interaction the replay does not reach.
--
--   G. THE REPLAYED RUN IS GRADED BY INDEPENDENT WITNESSES. Stage 3's
--      tautology control, carried forward and needed for the same reason: the
--      replay re-uses the recorder that produced the program, so a recorder gap
--      is on both sides of the comparison by construction.
--
--   H. S7's TWO OBLIGATIONS, ASSERTED AND NOT DESCRIBED. The surviving fixture
--      carries every shape the specification protects, and no two of its
--      quantities are equal. Seed 94 is that fixture: provider 11, approval
--      reads 10, approvals served 8, tool dispatches 3, tool ok 2, tool fault 1
--      — pairwise distinct, so no check can be reading one quantity while
--      claiming to read another.
--
--   I. MUTATION (C5), on every guard this stage adds — including the two
--      structural findings that keep axis A from being vacuous or count-shaped,
--      and both halves of S7. Each row mutates a REAL generated log or a real
--      witness, and the axis ends with the unmutated fixture surviving, because
--      mutation testing structurally cannot see a guard that fires too much.
--
--   J. DETERMINISM, last and least, and here it is worth less than usual: it is
--      the one property a seed-ignoring generator satisfies BEST.
--
--   K. D8's GENERATOR CANARY (stage 5). Pinned per stable generator id: if the
--      seed-to-choices mapping moves and `generator_version` does not, this
--      goes red. The pins are PURE — no driver is consulted — so a driver
--      change cannot produce a false alarm, because a canary that cries wolf
--      acquires a regeneration target, and a canary regenerated when it fails
--      certifies nothing while looking green forever. There is deliberately no
--      such target, no `--update` flag and no `ACCEPT=1`: a red canary has
--      exactly two legitimate outcomes, bump the version and re-pin by hand, or
--      fix the generator. The machinery and the pins live in `dst_generator`;
--      this axis ties them to the identity the suite actually runs under.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHY THE SEEDS ARE LITERALS, AND WHAT WOULD MAKE THEM STALE
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- 9, 13 and 94 were found by sweeping 1..260 and reading the class census of
-- every generated program. They are not arbitrary and they are not
-- interchangeable:
--
--   * 9 and 13 are the EQUAL-CENSUS pair axis A's anti-count control needs.
--     Their class censuses agree in every column — 14 interactions, 2 provider,
--     1 tool ok, 1 approval ok, 1 env ok, 2 chunked, 4 clock — so no count
--     anywhere in this suite can tell them apart and only the recorded
--     outcomes can.
--   * 94 is the S7 surviving fixture: of 260 seeds, exactly two reach every
--     protected shape with pairwise-distinct quantities and still terminate Ok
--     (68 and 94), and 94 is the richer of the two.
--
-- A change to the generator, to a request PROJECTION STRING, or to the driver's
-- control flow moves all of them, and the failure will be loud: axis H's shape
-- check or its distinctness check goes red and names the quantity that
-- collapsed. That is the intended behaviour and NOT a reason to relax the
-- check — re-sweep and re-pin. The whole point of asserting the fixture's
-- coverage rather than describing it is that the fixture drifts silently as the
-- driver changes and the author is the last person who will notice.

